Q: Adding HTTP security headers to WSO2 APIM (4.1.0) /service and /services endpoints A: <p>The <code>/service</code> and <code>/services</code> endpoints in WSO2 API Manager are handled
by the underlying <strong>Axis2</strong> engine, not the Synapse mediation engine —
which is why adding headers to the main/fault sequences doesn't affect them.</p>
<p>You have two options:</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Option 1 — Disable the <code>/services</code> endpoint entirely (recommended)</strong></p>
<p>If your APIs don't use SOAP/Axis2 services, the cleanest solution is to
disable these endpoints completely so they don't appear in security scans.</p>
<p>In &lt;APIM_HOME&gt;/repository/conf/deployment.toml, add:</p>
<pre class="lang-ini prettyprint-override"><code>[transport.http]
disable_wsdl_endpoint = true
</code></pre>
<p>This prevents the <code>/services</code> endpoint from being publicly accessible.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Option 2 — Add security headers via Axis2 handlers</strong></p>
<p>If you need to keep the endpoints active, you can add a custom Axis2
handler to inject security headers into responses from <code>/service</code>
and <code>/services</code>.</p>
<p>Create a custom handler extending <code>AbstractHandler</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class SecurityHeaderHandler extends AbstractHandler {
    public InvocationResponse invoke(MessageContext msgContext) 
            throws AxisFault {
        HttpServletResponse response = (HttpServletResponse) 
            msgContext.getProperty(
                HTTPConstants.MC_HTTP_SERVLETRESPONSE);
        if (response != null) {
            response.setHeader(&quot;X-Content-Type-Options&quot;, &quot;nosniff&quot;);
            response.setHeader(&quot;X-Frame-Options&quot;, &quot;DENY&quot;);
            response.setHeader(&quot;Strict-Transport-Security&quot;, 
                &quot;max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains&quot;);
            response.setHeader(&quot;Content-Security-Policy&quot;, 
                &quot;default-src 'none'&quot;);
        }
        return InvocationResponse.CONTINUE;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then register it in
<code>&lt;APIM_HOME&gt;/repository/conf/axis2/axis2.xml</code>
under the <code>&lt;phaseOrder type=&quot;OutFlow&quot;&gt;</code> section.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Option 3 — Block at the load balancer/reverse proxy</strong></p>
<p>If you have an Nginx or Apache reverse proxy in front of WSO2 APIM,
the simplest approach is to block <code>/service</code> and <code>/services</code> paths
entirely at the proxy level and add security headers there:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>location ~* ^/(service|services) {
    deny all;
    add_header X-Frame-Options &quot;DENY&quot;;
    add_header X-Content-Type-Options &quot;nosniff&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Option 1 is the cleanest for most production setups where SOAP
endpoints are not needed.</p>

Q: What is the best practice for avoiding (or handling) circular dependency in NestJS when needed for table relationships? A: <p>Ensure that your services are not causing circular dependencies. If your <code>SubjectService</code> depends on <code>SubSubjectService</code> and vice versa, you might need to use <code>forwardRef</code> in the service providers as well. Have you tried this?</p>
<pre><code>@Injectable()
export class SubjectService {
  constructor(
    @Inject(forwardRef(() =&gt; SubSubjectService))
    private readonly subSubjectService: SubSubjectService,
  ) {}
}
</code></pre>
